# Sometimes it might make sense.

If you're writing IoT chip or CPU software, I'll give you a pass.

As always, if you're in one of those very niche cases where you care about having as little memory/storage footprint as possible, understandable.
I'm not saying you're a bad person because you work in a domain where you're forced to implement those optimizations.
It might even be beneficial to follow the naming standards that are older than yourself - people using your software directly will appreciate it!
But that's the case where it makes sense.

You first solve the problem at hand, it works the way it should, but you would love it to fit in half the size.
You cut some unnecessary variables here, rewrite the code to be shorter there, but you're still missing that 1kB.
You *could* spend 2 more weeks looking for a better solution, rearchitecture the whole codebase and have it done.
You *could* also switch a few names for something shorter and be done with it this afternoon - knowing everything else works, so taking as little risk as possible.
